      "role": "user",
      "content": "idk the csp seems fine since other requests are working. i just tried looking at the raw json in the logs and it just says \"payment_intent.authentication_failure\" as the code, but it doesn't give much more than that. it feels like it's just being super vague. is there anything else in the request structure that could trigger this? like could it be a mismatch in currencies or something weird like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"payment_intent.authentication_failure\" error indicates an issue with the authentication when your request is sent to Stripe. Although you've checked your API key, it's worth confirming that you haven't accidentally exposed your Stripe secret key, and the public key is securely included in your client-side code or request headers only as needed.\n\nThe structure of your request should indeed match what Stripe expects, and mismatches in currency are unlikely to cause authentication issues specifically, but they can sometimes cause other types of errors. It's also crucial that the currency field, if included in your payload, matches one of the supported currencies listed in the Stripe documentation.\n\nConsider checking the exact structure of the headers and path parameters you've used in your request. Make sure that you haven’t inadvertently changed a parameter or added an unexpected response. Always refer back to the Stripe documentation when checking, as it provides the most reliable reference for required request parameters and structures. Given the limited information provided in the error code, you may need to employ error handling in your client or server code to log or alert you to these authentication failures, which might provide further insights. In the future, for logging and debugging, you may want to look for more verbose error handling or use Stripe's logs themselves via the dashboard to investigate ongoing",
